# Planar four-bar linkage (RBDA ch. 8): the canonical closed-loop test.
# Spanning tree = crank -> coupler -> rocker off the fixed base; the loop
# joint reconnects the rocker's far end to the ground pivot at (2, 0, 0).
#
# Ground g = 2.0, crank a = 0.5, coupler b = 1.5, rocker c = 1.2 satisfies
# Grashof (0.5 + 2.0 < 1.5 + 1.2), so the crank turns fully.
#
# The loop is closed with point-on-line, NOT revolute: a planar loop closed
# by a revolute is overconstrained (nc = 5, rank(K) = 2) and this pipeline
# requires properly constrained loops (see src/model/model.h). point-on-line
# about z constrains exactly the two in-plane translations at the pivot:
# nc = 2 = rank(K).
#
# n = 3 tree DOF - 2 constraints = mobility 1, as a four-bar should have.

gravity 0 0 -9.81
stab 0.05

body                     # 0: crank, ground pivot at the origin
parent -1
joint revolute z
tree 0 0 0
mass 1.0
cg 0.25 0 0
inertia 0.02 0.03 0.03 0 0 0
fext 1

body                     # 1: coupler, hangs off the crank tip
parent 0
joint revolute z
tree 0.5 0 0
mass 2.0
cg 0.75 0.05 0
inertia 0.05 0.4 0.4 0.01 0 0
fext 0

body                     # 2: rocker, hangs off the coupler tip
parent 1
joint revolute z
tree 1.5 0 0
mass 1.5
cg 0.6 0 0
inertia 0.03 0.2 0.2 0 0 0
fext 1

loop                     # rocker far end back to the ground pivot at (2,0,0)
predecessor -1
successor 2
joint point-on-line z
ptree 2.0 0 0
stree 1.2 0 0
